{
  "email": "sofia.rossi@brightline.example",
  "reason": "KB-012 quick check: see if the Q4 planning invite was quarantined"
}

{
  "outcome": "needs_user_info",
  "reply_to_user": "Hi Sofia, sorry you missed the Q4 planning invite. I checked, and it wasn't caught by the spam filter, and your mailbox has plenty of free space, so neither of those is the cause. A few quick things would help me narrow it down:\n1) Please sign in to Outlook on the web and check your inbox and calendar there. If the invite shows up on the web but not in the desktop Outlook app, it's a sync problem, and I can walk you through fixing it.\n2) Does anyone, such as an assistant or coworker, manage your calendar for you as a delegate? If so, invites may be going only to them.\n3) Who organized the meeting? Is it someone inside Brightline? Have you missed other invites from that same person before?\nWhile we look into it, the quickest way to get on the meeting is to ask the organizer to resend the invite or forward it to you. Let me know what you find and we'll take it from there.",
  "internal_note": "P3 email/calendar. User active, not VIP, mailbox 27.9% used. KB-012 quick check 1: quarantine is empty, so the invite was not held as spam. I have no tool to check delegate settings or the Outlook web vs desktop difference, so I asked the user about both. I also asked who the organizer is: if invites from one specific internal user never arrive, KB-012 says to escalate to Tier 2 (possible mail rule or transport issue). Interim fix: organizer resends the invite. If it turns out to be desktop-only, follow KB-009 sync steps. If a delegate is getting the invites, adjust the delegate settings so the user also gets copies.",
  "kb_articles": [
    "KB-012"
  ],
  "confidence": "medium"
}
